Spike/ReceiptSpike/fixtures/vision-ab/.raw/

# design/screenshots/ is deliberately NOT ignored: one screenshot per UI task is
# committed as the visual record of what that task actually produced
# (CLAUDE.md -> Conventions). They are evidence, not build output.

# Hugo site (site/): build output and resource cache
site/public/
site/resources/_gen/


# Backend runtime configuration: generated, never committed (2026-09-01).
# These carry real credentials - the S3 static key, the hash salt, the config
# signing seed - and this repo is public, where a committed secret stays in
# history after it is deleted. The committed *.template.json files beside them
# hold the full structure with the secret values blank, and
# `backend/scripts/generate-appsettings.sh` fills them from the environment.
#
# Measured, and the reason CI generates them rather than assuming they exist:
# with appsettings.json absent, `dotnet test` ABORTS after 117 of 295 tests.
# appsettings.Development.json is not load bearing (295/295 without it) but is
# generated too so a fresh clone works in one command.
backend/src/Tankbook.Api/appsettings.json
backend/src/Tankbook.Api/appsettings.Development.json

# `dotnet test --logger trx` output. The backend CI writes results.trx on the
# self-hosted runner to assert the Postgres-backed tests actually RAN - a suite
# that skips them still prints "Passed!" - and that artifact is never committed.
backend/**/TestResults/

# Release archives and exported IPAs (scripts/release.sh writes build/release-<n>-<sha>/).
# Ignored because the script REFUSES a dirty tree - the build number comes from
# the commit count, so archiving uncommitted work would produce a number that
# names nothing. Without this line the script's own output makes the tree dirty
# and blocks the next run, which is what happened on build 463.
build/

# Claude Code local state. `settings.local.json` is per-machine by design, and
# `worktrees/` is where other sessions check out branches inside this repo -
# one sat here during RV.52. Neither is this checkout's source, and leaving
# them untracked made `git status --porcelain` non-empty, which failed
# scripts/release.sh's dirty check (and made swiftlint walk a second copy of
# the tree). Ignored, not committed. `.claude/` itself stays free for shared
# config if we ever want it tracked.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/

# Generated extraction dumps. These come out of the diagnostics harness -
#   TANKBOOK_WRITE_CORPUS_FILES=1 TANKBOOK_DIAG_OUT=<dir> \
#     swift test --filter ReceiptFieldDiagnostics
# - and they rot the moment the parser changes: the copy committed with the
# 2026-09-04 merge still showed the three confident-wrong totals RV.56 had
# already fixed, so it misrepresented the parser it was meant to document.
# Spike/ReceiptSpike/fixtures/high-water.json is the durable record of what was
# measured; these are scratch. The hand-written research and plan .md files in
# the same directory ARE tracked and stay.
diagnostics/receipt-field-report.txt
diagnostics/receipt-ocr-lines.txt
diagnostics/pump-ocr-dump.txt
